WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.527
By the end of this module, I want you to be comfortable doing more than writing a WHERE clause.

00:00:08.527 --> 00:00:14.217
You should be able to look at a written business rule and translate it into SQL while being able to explain

00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:17.312
why the result is correct.

00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:24.067
You will practice combining Boolean conditions using AND, OR, and NOT.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:29.232
You will learn how SQL's three-valued logic works, meaning that expressions can evaluate to TRUE, FALSE,

00:00:29.232 --> 00:00:32.347
or UNKNOWN.

00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:39.382
You will see why NULL requires special treatment and why ordinary comparisons such as equals NULL do not work

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:42.327
the way beginners often expect.

00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:44.682
We will also use NULL-safe tools.

00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:49.482
IS NULL and IS NOT NULL let us test missingness directly.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:55.267
IS TRUE, IS FALSE, and IS UNKNOWN are useful for nullable Boolean fields.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:01:02.472
IS DISTINCT FROM gives us a definite comparison even when NULL is involved.

00:01:02.472 --> 00:01:08.752
COALESCE provides fallback values, and NULLIF helps normalize special values such as blank text or zero

00:01:08.752 --> 00:01:15.287
denominators. Another major objective is learning CASE expressions.

00:01:15.287 --> 00:01:20.602
CASE allows us to turn rules into labels such as High Risk, Standard, Needs Review, or No Charge.

00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:22.962
But CASE is order-sensitive.

00:01:22.962 --> 00:01:30.772
The first true branch wins, so broad conditions can accidentally hide more specific ones.

00:01:30.772 --> 00:01:34.722
Finally, I want you to validate your logic.

00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:38.587
Test NULL, zero, blank text, exact thresholds, and overlapping conditions.

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:46.106
In a real project, those edge cases are often where the most expensive mistakes happen.
